now even as the united states was  bringing in a new administration on  tuesday  china was slapping sanctions against the  old one  28 top officials of donald trump's  administration  including former secretary of state mike  pompeo was sanctioned by china for court  lying and cheating the measures have  been described as unproductive and  cynical  by biden administration officials the  new sanctions  underline the fraught relationship  between both countries  and if we needed any indication of how  it will develop over the next four years  president biden's nominee for secretary  of state anthony blinken  had this to say during his senate  confirmation hearing  i also believe that president trump was  right  in taking a tougher approach to china  i disagree very much with the way that  he went about it in a number of areas  but  the basic principle was the right one  and i think that's actually helpful  and there were other indications as well  from lincoln about the future u.s stance  on key issues with china  secretary pompeo designated the chinese  communist party as having  engaged in genocide regarding the uyghur  muslim population do you agree with that  that would be my judgment as well  you do agree yes there's been a strong  and long  bipartisan commitment to taiwan  the taiwan relations act also the  communiques  with china and part of that commitment  is making sure  that um taiwan have the ability  um to defend itself uh against  aggression  uh and that is a commitment that will uh  absolutely endure  uh in a uh an abiding industry for more  i'm joined now by professor steve tzang  he's director of the china institute  at the school of oriental and african  studies in london professor tsang  welcome  do you think we are looking at some sort  of a reset in ties between the us and  china  well the chinese government would like a  reset in u.s china relations  i think the washington side will see  a largely continuation of the  relationship  but a different style of managing it  when you said that the chinese  government is also looking for a reset  what do you mean by that  well the chinese government really has  been very unhappy with the  way how the trump administration has  been dealing with  china particularly in the last  few weeks of the trump administration  over initiatives on relaxing control  over  meetings between u.s officials and the  american officials as well as the  classification  of what happens in xinjiang as a kind of  genocide when it comes to the ties  between the united states  and china do you think that the  direction the future course of ties  between these two nations  will influence how the u.s approaches  its ties  with other countries in the region  i think it will  where i think we have is a new  administration in washington  which recognized that administrations  prior to the trump administration  had been less than entirely realistic in  dealing with china  and they didn't want to make the same  mistakes that previous administrations  have  made they also realized the trump  administration didn't  approach china very effectively  but they saw some of the basic strategic  thinking of the trump administration as  being  uh appropriate so they want to  keep to that and how the chinese will  respond to it  will affect how the americans feel that  they need to  react to china and engage with american  allies  in asia which are the same countries  that china wants to engage with  when you talk about this new sense of  realism  what shape might that realism take from  the biden administration  i think we have seen the uh the biola  administration  or indeed joe biden himself in his  inaugural  speech saying that valued  matters truth matters  and the chinese government has a  relationship with the truth  that is more similar to the way how the  trump administration had with truth  and that would pose a significant  problem for the  uh biden administration so it will take  a robust stance towards china  but it will also tries to engage with  china  to find ways to make things work  rather than take a confrontational  approach for the sake of it  i don't think there's any doubt or  argument that china plays a very very  important role and a very big one  in asia but help us understand professor  sang how  exactly china views its role in asia  well china really sees that is  long-term rifled place in asia  is to be the leader of asia  china would like itself to be perceived  in asia  as the benevolent big brother  and the other asian countries simply  running around  to support china and build up a  community under the leadership of china  now that may not be the way how a lot of  the countries in southeast asia  or indeed japan may see it but that's  how the chinese government would like it  to be  seeing as we are likely to see more  confrontation between the u.s and china  all be it in a form different to that  scene under the trump administration  are there any areas of cooperation  between the two countries  well there are areas where the two  countries can easily  cooperate for example uh on the issue of  climate change the byte administration  is reversing completely its approach on  this compared to the trump  administration  and therefore we should be quite  happy is to cooperate with the chinese  to move things forward that's not a  zero-sum game between the two countries  will the chinese government cooperate  with the biden administration  on climate change that remains to be  seen  will they be able to work out some kind  of a better economic relationship  rather than focus on the tariff war  against that remains to be seen  uh biden is not going to just dismantle  all the tariff regimes  in place he wants gustam's negotiate  it depends on how the chinese respond  interesting times ahead for us  professor steve tsang from sos in london  thank you so much sir for joining us  turning now to southeast asia where the  u.s has had a long-time military and  diplomatic presence  besides troops in many countries here  the united states has  formal defense arrangements with south  korea japan and the philippines  the military support is in a region with  significant security concerns  including china's claim over most of the  south china sea  the unilateral claims have often led to  a war of words  and small-scale confrontations on the  seas between chinese ships and those  from other countries  with competing claims in the region this  is in addition to long-running tensions  on the korean peninsula  which jointly impact relations between  the u.s  china japan and south korea  so let's break this down further joining  me now is sitting in pongs with iraq  he's director of the  institute of security and international  studies at chulalongkorn university in  bangkok welcome mr pongx with iraq i  realize it's very hard to generalize  about southeast asia and east asia  but do countries in the region have a  somewhat underlying common expectation  from the new u.s administration  yes i think broadly speaking the  southeast asian countries would like to  see more  reliability and dependability from u.s  administrations  the trump government the trump  administration has been a bit  erratic you know we had a trade war with  china  technology conflict and so on so i think  there's a lot of  anticipation about new administration  that maybe  can provide more more balance more peace  and stability in the regions  and maybe a bit of a new chapter because  i think it's been very tumultuous uh  during the term period  however uh the u.s china trade  technology conflict  has been viewed favorably in southeast  asia so i think what southeast asia  would like to see is that  it's some kind of a balance you know  between maybe the obama time and the  trump administration  under obama i think the u.s left a lot  to be desired in the region to  raise a lot of expectations but  unfulfilled with the pivot and rebalance  and china kind of took over the south  china sea during that time  trump went the other way very  belligerent and a bit erratic and  unpredictable  so i think maybe violent we hope will be  something in between speaking of balance  uh you have china that looms large in  southeast asia as you also  pointed out do countries in the region  have this unique challenge to balance  the relationship with the us  with ties with china even though many  have territorial disputes and  disagreements with china  yeah countries in the region do not want  to be dominated  by by china generally speaking or by any  major power i think that the region  would like to see  some tension between us and china  because that allows  leverage uh you know for the the asian  smaller states at the same time they  don't want to see an outright war on  conflict uh  you know even the trade war that we've  seen has been very  uh a bit unstable unstable and so on so  we would like to see something in  between  um i think for china uh it's a  residential  super power i think the the the smaller  states would like to see some kind of  balance  between the two u.s and china speaking  of china again china plays an important  role in at least  two issues that are long running in the  region one is the south china sea  dispute  and also it has a role to play uh when  it comes to tensions  on the korean peninsula and what it can  do to potentially calm them  but are we likely to see any changes in  the u.s  approach to these areas of concern  it remains to be seen but the the people  around  president biden seem to come from the  obama period  so i think the biden administration will  be tested very early on  by north korea we already see some  challenges there and also i think the  chinese also will test the biden  administration the way it did with the  obama administration  and then for for southeast asia south  china sea is a major hot spot  but also in mainland southeast asia the  medical regions  around the mekong river where china has  built these upstream dams  unilaterally is also a major issue so i  think  across the region from northeast asia to  southeast asia a number of hot spots a  lot of  contentious issues by the administration  that will be  watched very carefully what kind of  people they put in charge of the region  i think that they will be more attentive  to the region we already  see some some hopeful optimistic words  from the uh people like kurt campbell  and so on so the early signs are good  from the  demonstrations and maybe they can be  something like you know not obama 2.0  but maybe this is a second  chance for the wider administration to  do what the obama administration tried  to do but did not succeed  an interesting space to watch but thank  you so much for joining us today 39  points with iraq

